.TH SHIT 1 "2026-08-04" "shit" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
shit \- a very fast and debuggable POSIX- and Bash-compatible command shell
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B shit
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ script
.RI [ argument ...]]
.br
.B shit
.B \-c
.I command
.RI [ argument ...]
.br
.B shit
.B \-s
.RI [ argument ...]
.PP
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B shit
is an interpreter, interactive shell and a diagnostic tool with full Bash and
POSIX shell syntax and Bash-compatible extensions.
.PP
It aims to be a complete, faster and portable Bash-compatible shell replacement
for systems, runners or development machines that can benefit from it's speed
and pedantic diagnostics.
.PP
The shell is friendly to coding agents. Strict validation and source-located
error messages provide clear feedback while shell commands are generated or
repaired.
.PP
The shell gurantees first tier support for Linux, macOS and Windows,
preserving equivalent behavior on either of these systems.
.PP
An interactive shell is started when standard input is a terminal and no
.BR \-s ,
.BR \-c ,
or script operand is given. A script operand names a file to run. The
.B \-c
option supplies a command string. The
.B \-s
option reads commands from standard input.
.PP
Shell source accepts LF and CRLF line endings. A CRLF pair is read as one
newline. A single carriage return remains part of the source text.
.PP
The grammar is the same on every operating system. An unquoted backslash is an
escape on Windows. A native Windows path therefore uses a quoted backslash, an
escaped backslash, or a forward slash. After quote removal, Windows path
operations accept both slash forms and recognize drive and UNC roots. POSIX
path operations accept only a forward slash.
.SH INVOCATION
The input source is selected in this order. The
.B \-s
option selects standard input. One or more
.B \-c
options select their command strings in occurrence order. A script operand
selects that file. The
.B \-i
option or a terminal on standard input selects the interactive editor when no
earlier source applies. Standard input is used otherwise.
.PP
When both
.B \-s
and
.B \-i
are present, a terminal on standard input selects interactive operation. A
non-terminal selects standard input. The
.B \-s
option takes precedence over command strings and file operands. Command strings
take precedence over
.BR \-i .
A script operand takes precedence over
.BR \-i .
.PP
The invocation basename selects an initial mood. The names
.B sh
and
.B dash
select the
.B sh
mood. The names
.B bash
and
.B rbash
select the
.B bash
mood. The name
.B rbash
also requests restricted mode. A dash at the start of argument zero or the
.B \-l
option selects login operation.
.PP
The
.B SHIT_FLAGS
environment variable supplies options before the command line is parsed. A
command-line option overrides the corresponding value from
.BR SHIT_FLAGS .
The
.B \-c
option is discarded from
.B SHIT_FLAGS
and cannot inject a command string.
.PP
A dash-prefixed invocation name whose options cannot be parsed enters rescue
mode when standard input is a terminal. Rescue mode clears the options and
skips startup files. The
.B \-l
option alone does not enable this recovery. Another invalid invocation exits
with status 2.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Input and invocation
.TP
.BR \-c " " \fICOMMAND\fR ", " \-\-command =\fICOMMAND\fR
COMMAND is read and run. The option may be repeated. Command strings run in
occurrence order.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-stdin
Commands are read from standard input.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-interactive
Interactive operation is requested.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-login
Login operation is requested.
.TP
.B \-\-
Option parsing ends. The next operand is treated as the script name.
.SS Mood and startup
.TP
.BR \-M ", " \-\-mood =\fINAME\fR
NAME selects
.BR shit ,
.BR bash ,
.BR sh ,
or
.BR bash-posix .
.TP
.BR \-L ", " \-\-init\-moods =\fILIST\fR
Startup files for each listed mood are sourced in order. LIST accepts
comma-separated names. The option may be repeated. The selected runtime mood
is used when the option is omitted.
.TP
.BR \-I ", " \-\-mimicry
A script with a recognized shell shebang runs under the corresponding mood.
The current diagnostic setting is retained.
.TP
.B \-\-dumb
The options
.BR "\-\-mood sh" ,
.BR \-T ,
and
.B \-\-no\-diagnostics
are applied. Color is disabled.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-privileged
Privileged mode is enabled. BASH_ENV is not sourced. When the real and
effective ids differ, every startup file is skipped. Elevated ids are retained
only when this option is present. The state is exposed through
.BR $\- ,
.BR SHELLOPTS ,
and
.BR "set \-o privileged" .
.TP
.B \-\-clean
No startup file is read. PATH is set to /usr/bin:/bin.
.TP
.BR \-\-init\-file " " \fIFILE\fR ", " \-\-rcfile " " \fIFILE\fR
FILE selects the interactive rc file. The last occurrence of either alias wins.
A leading tilde is expanded. The file replaces /etc/shitrc and ~/.shitrc in the
.B shit
mood. The file replaces ~/.bashrc in an interactive non-login Bash mood. The
system Bash rc is still read first.
.TP
.B \-\-norc
The interactive non-login Bash rc and a custom rc file are skipped. Login
profiles and noninteractive BASH_ENV processing are unchanged. The stock Bash
completion script is not loaded.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-restricted
Restricted mode is requested. Startup files run before the restrictions become
active.
.TP
.B \-\-posix
The
.B bash-posix
mood is selected.
.SS Shell operation
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-error\-exit
The shell exits after an unguarded failing command. A function called from a
non-final operand of
.B &&
or
.BR || ,
or from a negation, inherits the guarded condition for its complete body.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-no\-glob
Pathname expansion is disabled.
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-no\-unset
An unset variable read is an error.
.TP
.BR \-C ", " \-\-no\-clobber
An existing file is not overwritten by a plain output redirection.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-no\-exec
Commands are parsed without being run. A runtime
.B set \-n
also suppresses later commands in the current command list.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-one\-command
One command is read and run. The shell then exits.
.TP
.BR \-x ", " \-\-xtrace
Each command is printed after expansion.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Each input line is printed as it is read.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-export\-all
Each later assignment is marked for export.
.TP
.B \-h
The option is accepted without effect for compatibility.
.TP
.B \-m
The option is accepted without effect for compatibility.
.SS Diagnostics
.TP
.B \-W[W...]
The analysis stage runs. One
.B \-W
demotes a lenient analysis error to a warning. An unresolved command and a
variable read before assignment are lenient errors. A strict analysis error
remains fatal. The form
.B \-WW
demotes every analysis error to a warning. Runtime warnings are reported in the
default mood under
.B \-W
and in every mood under
.BR \-WW .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-diagnostics
The analysis stage is skipped. No warning or pre-run diagnostic is reported.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-init\-diagnostics
Diagnostics and warnings are suppressed while startup files are sourced. The
previous settings are restored before interactive input begins.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-traces
Source backtraces are suppressed. The primary message, source caret, note, and
detail location remain visible when available.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-diagnostics
The checks reported by the analysis stage are listed. The shell then exits.
The checks cover unsafe word splitting and globbing, misleading quoting,
command-output iteration, test and conditional mistakes, redirection order and
privilege boundaries, pipeline state loss, unvalidated arithmetic input,
unreachable control flow, nonportable syntax, and dangerous file operations.
The source is also checked for a UTF-8 byte-order mark before the shebang or
first command.
.SS Interactive operation
.TP
.BR \-T ", " \-\-no\-completion
Tab completion and ghost text are disabled.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-syntax\-highlighting
Syntax coloring and ghost text are disabled. Tab completion remains enabled.
.SS Shitbox
.TP
.B \-\-enable\-shitbox
Bundled utility names resolve directly as commands. The option is equivalent to
.BR "set \-o shitbox" .
.SS Information
.TP
.B \-\-help
Help is displayed. The shell then exits.
.TP
.B \-\-version
The version and notices are displayed. The shell then exits.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-short\-version
The short version is displayed. The shell then exits.
.SS Debugging
.TP
.BR \-A ", " \-\-show\-ast
The syntax tree is printed before each command runs.
.TP
.B \-\-show\-optimizer\-state
The analysis prepass decisions are traced. A located line is printed for every
eliminated node.
.TP
.BR \-E ", " \-\-show\-exit\-code
The exit status is printed after each command.
.TP
.BR \-R ", " \-\-show\-lexed\-words
The escape bitmap is printed after each parsed command.
.TP
.B \-\-show\-stats
Command, expansion, node, and arena statistics are printed after each command.
.TP
.B \-\-show\-memory
Arena and heap usage is printed at exit.
.TP
.BR \-X ", " \-\-debug\-logging =\fILEVEL\fR
Internal logging is enabled at
.BR info ,
.BR debug ,
or
.BR all .
An unknown level is a usage error. This option is available only in a debug
build.
.TP
.B \-\-debug\-logging\-file=FILE
Debug logging is appended to FILE. A missing file is created. Standard error is
used when the option is omitted or the file cannot be opened. This option is
available only in a debug build.
.TP
.B \-\-debug\-complete\-at=LINE
Completion candidates for LINE are printed. The shell then exits. This option
is available only in a debug build.
.TP
.B \-\-debug\-highlight\-at=LINE
Highlight spans for LINE are printed. The shell then exits. This option is
available only in a debug build.
.TP
.B \-\-debug\-ghost\-at=LINE
The ghost completion result and operation counts for LINE are printed. The
shell then exits. This option is available only in a debug build.
.TP
.BR \-\-ftrace ", " \-\-strace
Function or system-call tracing is enabled in a Cosmopolitan build. These
options are unavailable in other builds.
.SH OPERANDS
.TP
.I script
When neither
.B \-s
nor
.B \-c
selects another source, the first operand names the script file. A lone
.B \-
operand selects standard input. A directory operand fails with status 126. A
file that cannot be opened fails with status 127.
.TP
.I argument
For a script invocation, the script pathname becomes $0. Arguments after the
script become positional parameters. For a
.B \-c
invocation, the first operand after COMMAND becomes $0. Later operands become
positional parameters. For
.B \-s
and interactive operation, $0 remains the invocation name and every operand
becomes a positional parameter.
.SH MOODS
The
.B shit
mood enables strict checks, pipefail, failglob, nounset, and analysis by
default. The
.B bash
mood accepts Bash extensions with analysis disabled by default. The
.B sh
mood follows the supported dash behavior. The
.B bash-posix
mood uses Bash syntax with the POSIX option enabled and reads the Bash startup
files.
.PP
An explicit
.BR "set \-u" ,
.BR "set \-o pipefail" ,
or
.B set \-o failglob
survives a later mood change. The command
.B set \-o posix
selects
.BR bash-posix .
The command
.B set +o posix
selects
.B bash
only from the
.B bash-posix
or
.B sh
mood.
.PP
On Windows, the default mood leaves backslashes literal in
.BR echo .
The
.B \-e
option enables escape decoding. The
.B sh
mood retains dash-style default escape handling.
.SH STARTUP FILES
Each startup file is parsed with the grammar of the mood whose files are being
sourced. A mood already being initialized is skipped if a startup file invokes
.BR "set \-\-init\-moods" .
.PP
A login
.B shit
or
.B sh
mood sources /etc/profile and ~/.profile in that order. An interactive
.B shit
mood then sources /etc/shitrc and ~/.shitrc. A custom rc file replaces that
pair.
.PP
An interactive
.B sh
mood sources the file named by ENV when privileged mode is disabled. The ENV
value is used as a pathname without PATH lookup.
.PP
A login
.B bash
or
.B bash-posix
mood sources /etc/profile. It then sources the first readable file among
~/.bash_profile, ~/.bash_login, and ~/.profile.
.PP
An interactive non-login
.B bash
or
.B bash-posix
mood sources the first readable file among /etc/bash/bashrc and
/etc/bash.bashrc. It then sources the custom rc file or ~/.bashrc. Unless a
default completion specification or BASH_COMPLETION_VERSINFO already exists,
/usr/share/bash-completion/bash_completion is sourced after the rc file.
.PP
A noninteractive
.B bash
mood sources BASH_ENV once. Parameter expansion, command substitution,
arithmetic expansion, and leading-tilde expansion are applied to the value.
PATH is not searched. The
.B bash-posix
mood does not source BASH_ENV.
.PP
Clean, rescue, and unequal-id shells skip every startup file. The privileged
option suppresses ENV and BASH_ENV processing. Restricted mode is visible while
startup files run, but its restrictions become active only after startup
finishes.
.PP
Mood-derived pipefail and failglob settings remain disabled while startup files
run. An explicit command-line nounset setting remains active. The selected mood
strictness is applied after startup finishes.
.PP
Missing optional files, custom rc files, and BASH_ENV files are skipped. An
existing custom rc or BASH_ENV file that cannot be read produces a diagnostic
and is skipped. An unreadable ENV file is skipped without that diagnostic. See
.BR shit (5)
for the startup file format.
.SH RESTRICTED SHELL
A restricted shell rejects directory changes and changes to SHELL, PATH,
HISTFILE, ENV, and BASH_ENV. A command name may not contain a directory
separator. A source operand, history path, and
.B hash \-p
path may not contain a directory separator.
.PP
The output redirections >, >|, <>, >>, &>, &>>, and output redirection through
>& are rejected. Numeric descriptor duplication remains available. The
.B exec
builtin,
.BR "command \-p" ,
and
.B enable \-f
or
.B enable \-d
are rejected. Restricted mode cannot be disabled with
.BR "set +r" .
Changes to the restricted_shell shopt value are accepted without effect.
.PP
The restrictions become active after startup files finish. A sourced script
retains them. A shell script found through PATH and executed as a command runs
without them.
.SH SHELL GRAMMAR
The shell accepts the POSIX Shell Command Language and the Bash extensions
described below. The
.B sh
mood rejects Bash-only syntax. Compatibility differences are listed under
.BR COMPATIBILITY .
.PP
The analysis prepass may fold constants and remove an unreachable command. A
C-style for loop whose condition folds to zero is removed only when its init
clause is empty. A nonempty init clause still runs once.
.PP
An if command with no selected branch and no else command returns status zero.
This rule also applies at the end of a sourced file.
.SH QUOTING
Single quotes preserve every enclosed byte. Double quotes retain parameter,
command, and arithmetic expansion. A backslash quotes the next byte where the
grammar permits it.
.PP
The form $'...' reads C escapes including \en, \et, \exHH, and \eOOO. The form
$"..." marks a string for translation. It behaves as an ordinary double-quoted
string when no catalog is present.
.SH PARAMETERS
.SS Positional parameters
An unquoted $@ or $* is subject to field splitting. The form "$@" preserves one
field per positional parameter. The form "$*" joins the parameters with the
first byte of IFS.
.SS Indexed arrays
An indexed array is assigned with a=(x y z). An element is assigned with
a[2]=z. The form a+=(w) appends elements. ${a[i]} reads an arithmetic subscript.
${a[@]} and ${a[*]} list elements. ${#a[@]} counts elements. ${!a[@]} lists
indices. A negative index counts from the end. Sparse indices remain absent
from ${!a[@]}.
.PP
Inside double quotes, "${a[@]}" preserves one field per element.
"${a[*]}" joins elements with the first byte of IFS. An unset array read through
${a[@]} is an error under
.BR "set \-u" .
.SS Associative arrays
The command
.B declare \-A m
declares an associative array. The form m[key]=v assigns an element. The form
${m[key]} reads it. The form ${!m[@]} lists keys. The command
.B unset 'm[key]'
removes one element.
.SH EXPANSIONS
.SS Parameter expansion
The form ${v:offset:length} selects a substring. A negative offset requires a
leading space, as in ${v: \-3}. Without the space, ${v:\-3} is the default-value
form. A negative length excludes that many characters from the end.
.PP
The form ${v/pat/rep} replaces the first match. The form ${v//pat/rep} replaces
every match. The anchors /# and /% select the start and end. An ampersand in the
replacement denotes the matched text. The form \e& denotes a literal ampersand.
.PP
The forms ${v^^}, ${v,,}, ${v^}, and ${v,} change case with an optional pattern.
The transforms ${v@Q}, ${v@E}, ${v@A}, ${v@P}, and ${v@a} produce a quoted
value, expanded escapes, an assignment, a prompt expansion, or attributes. The
forms ${v@U}, ${v@L}, and ${v@u} apply the corresponding case conversion.
.PP
The form ${!ref} reads the variable named by ref. The forms ${!pre*} and
${!pre@} list names beginning with pre.
.SS Arithmetic expansion
Arithmetic accepts the C operators, ++, \-\-, **, the conditional operator, the
comma operator, and assignment operators. Literals may use 0x hexadecimal, 0
octal, 0b binary, or a base prefix such as 16#ff. A variable value is
re-evaluated as an arithmetic expression. Nested subscripts such as a[b[c]] are
resolved from the inner expression outward. An empty (( )) command returns one.
An empty $(( )) expansion produces zero.
.SS Command substitution
The forms $(command) and `command` replace a command with its output. A command
substitution restores runtime state, shell options, directory stack, working
directory state, and file creation mask when it finishes.
.SS Process substitution
The forms <(command) and >(command) supply a descriptor path. A POSIX process
evaluates the inherited syntax tree. A Windows process uses the platform
fallback selected for that command.
.SS Brace expansion
The form {a,b,c} produces alternatives. The forms {1..10}, {a..z}, and
{1..10..2} produce ranges. A numeric range such as {001..10} retains its width.
Braces nest. A lone {a} remains literal.
.SS Field splitting
IFS controls field splitting. A run of an IFS whitespace byte collapses. A run
of a non-whitespace IFS byte produces an empty field where applicable.
.SS Pathname expansion
The
.B extglob
option enables ?(), *(), +(), @(), and !(). The
.B globstar
option enables **. The
.BR dotglob ,
.BR nullglob ,
and
.B failglob
options control leading dots, nonmatches, and failed matches. In the default
mood, a nonmatching glob is fatal. In a compatibility mood with failglob
disabled, the nonmatching pattern remains literal. The warning policy may
report that literal fallback. A glob in command position is fatal in the
default mood and reports a warning in a compatibility mood unless diagnostics
are disabled.
.SH REDIRECTIONS
The operators &> and &>> redirect standard output and standard error to one
file. The operator |& pipes both streams. The operator <<< supplies a
here-string followed by one newline.
.PP
A redirection of the form {var}> or {var}< allocates a descriptor numbered ten
or greater and assigns its number to var. The form {var}>&\- closes the
descriptor named by var.
.PP
A contiguous here-document retains its source location. A tab-stripped
here-document has no source mapping because its collected bytes are not
contiguous.
.SH COMMAND EXECUTION
Command lookup checks functions, builtins, remembered command paths, PATH, and
enabled shitbox fallback according to the active mood and options. Completion
indexes do not populate the execution hash. Actual execution and the
.B hash
builtin remember a resolved program path. A PATH directory membership change
invalidates the derived indexes and execution hash.
.PP
A command path ending in a directory separator fails with status 126 when the
typed target is not a directory. Components are resolved in order. An
unavailable path is reported through its first unavailable component. The caret
covers that component.
.PP
Executable-format fallback starts a fresh evaluator with the command
environment and command argument zero. Caller variables, functions, and traps
do not leak into it. Each complete top-level command runs before the next one is
parsed.
.PP
PIPESTATUS is updated after every foreground command. It contains the status of
each pipeline stage. When
.B pipefail
is enabled, a pipeline returns the status of its rightmost failing stage.
.SH FUNCTIONS
A function has its own positional parameters and local variables. Call stack
state is exposed through FUNCNAME, BASH_SOURCE, BASH_LINENO, and BASH_COMMAND.
.PP
A function called from a guarded and-or operand or a negation inherits that
condition. The
.B errexit
option remains suppressed throughout the guarded function body.
.SH BUILTINS
The shell provides the POSIX and Bash builtins listed below. The standard forms
follow the active compatibility mood unless a difference is stated here.
.PP
.BR : ,
.BR . ,
.BR [ ,
.BR alias ,
.BR bg ,
.BR break ,
.BR builtin ,
.BR cd ,
.BR command ,
.BR complete ,
.BR compgen ,
.BR compopt ,
.BR continue ,
.BR declare ,
.BR dirs ,
.BR disown ,
.BR echo ,
.BR eval ,
.BR exec ,
.BR exit ,
.BR export ,
.BR false ,
.BR fc ,
.BR fg ,
.BR getopts ,
.BR history ,
.BR jobs ,
.BR kill ,
.BR let ,
.BR local ,
.BR mapfile ,
.BR newgrp ,
.BR popd ,
.BR printf ,
.BR pushd ,
.BR pwd ,
.BR read ,
.BR readarray ,
.BR readonly ,
.BR return ,
.BR set ,
.BR shift ,
.BR shopt ,
.BR source ,
.BR test ,
.BR time ,
.BR times ,
.BR trap ,
.BR true ,
.BR type ,
.BR typeset ,
.BR ulimit ,
.BR umask ,
.BR unalias ,
.BR unset ,
and
.BR wait .
.SS Shell-specific builtins
.TP
.BI "assimilate " TARGET
The running executable is copied through scp to TARGET and installed as
.B shit
in the first usable remote PATH directory. A path containing sbin is skipped.
/usr/local/bin and $HOME/.local/bin are preferred when they occur in PATH. The
.BI \-\-link\-mood= MOOD
option requests a relative bash, dash, sh, or shit link. It may be repeated and
accepts comma-separated values. A candidate directory is skipped when a
requested link path already exists.
.PP
The
.BR \-x " or " \-\-trace
option prints remote progress. The
.BI \-\-ssh\-command= COMMAND
and
.BI \-\-scp\-command= COMMAND
options replace ssh and scp. COMMAND is split at literal spaces.
.PP
The candidate identity is validated before the target is replaced. A handled
failure restores the prior file or symlink and removes transaction files.
Concurrent transactions are serialized. A later transaction recovers a
published or orphaned journal. A failure before the remote transaction starts
cannot alter the installed target. An unusable partial upload may remain.
.TP
.B bench
Commands are sampled through
.BR "shit \-c" .
The wall time, peak resident set, and available performance counters are
reported as a mean, standard deviation, and range. Later commands are compared
with the first. Sampling stops after a nonzero status unless
.B \-\-ignore\-exit\-code
is present. The
.BR \-\-show\-output ,
.BR \-\-no\-shell ,
.BR \-\-runs ,
and
.B \-\-duration
options control output, direct execution, sample count, and the millisecond
budget.
.TP
.B z
The current directory is changed to a directory selected from the frecency
store. Operands form one case-insensitive substring query. Visit count and
recency determine the ranking.
.SS Compatibility forms
.TP
.B set
A minus enables an option. A plus disables it. The forms
.BR "set \-o NAME" " and " "set +o NAME"
select the option by long name. With no NAME,
.B set \-o
prints current states and
.B set +o
prints reusable commands. The
.B set \-\-options
form prints every option with its state and description. The
.BR \-E ,
.BR \-P ,
.BR \-T ,
and
.B \-B
forms control errtrace, physical directory resolution, functrace, and brace
expansion. The custom forms
.BR "set \-\-mood" " and " "set \-\-init\-moods"
use the same mood table as the command-line options. The options
.BR force-warnings ,
.BR force-diagnostics ,
.BR no-diagnostics ,
and
.B shitbox
control diagnostics and bundled command lookup. Runtime snapshots restore set
state after an in-process substitution.
.TP
.B enable
Every builtin remains enabled. The
.BR \-d ,
.BR \-n ,
.BR \-f ,
and
.B \-s
compatibility flags are accepted without effect. The
.B \-a
option lists every builtin.
.TP
.B hash
Resolved command locations are remembered or listed. The
.B \-r
option clears the cache. The
.BI "\-p " path
option records path as the location of each name.
.TP
.B history
The
.B \-r
option reads the history file. The
.B \-w
option writes it. The
.B \-c
option clears the stored history.
.TP
.B "fc [\-e editor] [\-lnr] [first [last]]"
The selected history events are edited and executed in the current shell. A
decimal selector names an event number. A negative selector is relative to the
current history position. A text selector finds the newest event with that
prefix. The first and last selectors form an inclusive range. Without
selectors, editing selects the previous event.
.PP
The
.B \-l
option lists events without executing them. A list without selectors contains
up to the previous sixteen events. The
.B \-n
option omits event numbers, and the
.B \-r
option reverses the selected order.
.PP
The editor is selected by
.B \-e
and then by FCEDIT and EDITOR. The default is vi, or ed in POSIX mood. A dash
as the editor skips editing and executes the selected event. The editor receives
the temporary file path as its final argument.
.PP
The form
.B "fc \-s [old=new ...] [command]"
selects one event and applies each literal replacement in order. The result is
printed to standard error and executed. The active fc event is replaced by the
executed command. The return status is the status of the executed command.
.TP
.B bind
Flags and operands are accepted without changing key bindings. The builtin
returns zero.
.TP
.B caller
Without an operand, the builtin returns one and prints nothing at top level. It
returns zero inside a function or sourced file. A numeric operand selects a
call frame and prints its line number and source file. A nonnumeric operand is
an error.
.TP
.B help
Without a pattern, builtin names are printed one per line. A builtin name
renders the same help as its
.B \-\-help
flag. An unknown name is an error. The
.B \-d
and
.B \-m
flags are accepted without effect.
.TP
.B logout
A login shell runs the EXIT trap and exits. An optional operand supplies the
status. A non-login shell reports an error and returns one.
.TP
.B suspend
The shell stops on SIGSTOP and resumes on SIGCONT. A login shell rejects the
operation unless
.B \-f
is present.
.TP
.BR "declare, local, typeset, readonly"
These builtins accept
.BR \-a ,
.BR \-A ,
.BR \-i ,
.BR \-r ,
and
.BR \-x .
.TP
.BR "mapfile, readarray"
The
.B \-t
option reads input lines into an array without their delimiters.
.TP
.B printf
The
.B \-v
option assigns the formatted result to a variable.
.TP
.B read
The
.B \-r
option retains backslashes. A plain read treats a trailing backslash as a line
continuation.
.SH SHITBOX UTILITIES
The
.B shitbox
builtin runs a bundled utility. An unknown utility is an error. Every utility
supports
.BR \-\-help .
A utility invoked through a matching executable name also supports
.BR \-\-version .
.PP
The command
.BI "shitbox \-\-assimilate " DIR
installs one symlink per utility into DIR. When direct shitbox lookup is
enabled, a PATH program takes precedence over a bundled utility.
.TP
.B basename
The final path component is printed. An optional suffix operand is removed.
.TP
.B calc
Arithmetic operands are evaluated. The
.B \-i
and
.B \-p
options read expressions interactively or from a pipe.
.TP
.B cat
Files or standard input are concatenated. The
.B \-n
option numbers lines. The
.B \-\-syntax\-highlighting
option colors recognized shell source when standard output is a terminal. A
file containing a null byte is written without highlighting. Line numbering
continues across file and standard input boundaries.
.TP
.B cp
Files and directory trees are copied with
.BR \-r ,
.BR \-R ,
and
.BR \-v .
.TP
.B dirname
The directory portion of one path is printed.
.TP
.B du
Path sizes are totaled without following symbolic links. The
.B \-s
and
.B \-h
options are supported.
.TP
.B env
Temporary assignments are applied before a command, or the environment is
printed.
.TP
.B find
Sorted trees are walked with
.BR \-name ,
.BR \-type ,
.BR \-maxdepth ,
.BR \-mindepth ,
and
.BR \-print .
.TP
.B flock
A command runs while a directory lock is held.
.TP
.B grep
Regular-expression matches are printed. The
.B \-i
and
.B \-v
options are supported.
.TP
.B head
The first ten lines are printed by default. The
.B \-n
and
.B \-c
options select a count.
.TP
.B tail
The last ten lines are printed by default. The
.B \-n
and
.B \-c
options select a count.
.TP
.B killall
Processes are signaled by exact name. Signal selection and listing use
.BR \-s ,
.BR \-\-signal ,
.BR \-l ,
and
.BR \-\-list .
.TP
.B pkill
Processes whose names match a pattern are signaled. Signal selection and
listing use
.BR \-s ,
.BR \-\-signal ,
.BR \-l ,
and
.BR \-\-list .
.TP
.B ln
Symbolic links are created with
.B \-s
and
.BR \-f .
.TP
.B ls
Sorted entries are listed with
.BR \-a ,
.BR \-A ,
.BR \-1 ,
.BR \-l ,
and
.BR \-h .
.TP
.B make
Compact make rules are run. The
.BR \-f ,
.BR \-\-file ,
.BR \-C ,
and
.B \-\-directory
options are supported. The unused
.B \-B
and
.B \-k
options are accepted. The
.B \-j
option is ignored.
.TP
.B mkdir
Directories are created with
.B \-p
and an octal
.B \-m
mode.
.TP
.B mv
Sources are renamed with
.B \-f
and
.BR \-v .
.TP
.B nproc
The available logical processor count is printed. The
.B \-\-all
and
.B \-\-ignore=COUNT
options are supported.
.TP
.B ps
Processes are listed with
.BR \-a ,
.BR \-u ,
.BR \-x ,
and the accepted
.B \-w
option.
.TP
.B realpath
The canonical absolute form of each path is printed.
.TP
.B rm
Paths are removed with
.BR \-r ,
.BR \-R ,
.BR \-f ,
and
.BR \-\-dry\-run .
Root, dot, and dot-dot are refused.
.TP
.B rmdir
Empty directories are removed.
.TP
.B seq
Signed integer sequences are printed with a nonzero step.
.TP
.B sleep
Execution pauses for summed durations with optional s, m, h, or d suffixes.
.TP
.B sort
Input lines are sorted in byte order. The
.B \-r
option reverses the result.
.TP
.B tee
Standard input is copied to output files. The
.B \-a
option appends.
.TP
.B timeout
A command is supervised for a suffixed duration. The
.BR \-s ,
.BR \-\-signal ,
.BR \-k ,
.BR \-\-kill\-after ,
.BR \-p ,
and
.B \-\-preserve\-status
options control signals and status handling.
.TP
.B touch
File times are updated. The
.B \-c
option avoids creating a missing file.
.TP
.B tr
Input bytes are translated with ranges and character classes. The
.B \-d
option deletes bytes.
.TP
.B uniq
Adjacent equal lines are collapsed. The
.B \-c
option prints run counts.
.TP
.B unlink
One non-directory path is removed.
.TP
.B wc
Lines, words, and bytes are counted with
.BR \-l ,
.BR \-w ,
and
.BR \-c .
.TP
.B which
Builtins, PATH programs, and shitbox utilities are resolved. The
.BR \-a ,
.BR \-\-all ,
.BR \-q ,
and
.B \-\-quiet
options are supported.
.TP
.B whoami
The current user name is printed.
.TP
.B yes
The joined operands, or y when none are given, are repeated until output
closes.
.SH LINE EDITING
The interactive editor starts in emacs mode. The command
.B set \-o vi
selects vi mode. The command
.B set \-o emacs
restores emacs mode.
.PP
The shell does not set the terminal window title.
.PP
Ghost text uses prefix matches from completion and history. Tab also offers
smart-case and subsequence matches. An empty command position includes
builtins, functions, aliases, shitbox utilities, and programs from PATH. An
exact prefix ranks before a subsequence match. Empty ghost completion does not
scan PATH.
.PP
Completion handles quoted separators, command substitutions, and here-document
bodies when it selects the command segment at the cursor. Quoted, escaped,
variable, and tilde directory prefixes retain their typed form. An explicit Tab
at command position offers executable files and directories. Syntax
highlighting distinguishes syntax, commands, flags, variables, paths, globs,
and invalid input.
.PP
Each vi line begins in insert mode. Escape enters command mode. Command mode
supports motions, counted operators, named registers, change repeat, and visual
selection. The
.B v
key selects characters. The
.B V
key selects whole lines.
.PP
The
.B u
key undoes the last edit in command mode. Ctrl-_ undoes it in either mode.
Ctrl-^ redoes the edit in either mode. Ctrl-R redoes it in command mode.
Ctrl-R opens incremental history search in insert mode. The slash key opens the
same search in command mode. Enter accepts the highlighted match without
running it. Ctrl-G cancels the search and restores the preceding line state.
.PP
The colon key opens an ex command. The commands q, q!, wq, wq!, x, and quit
exit the shell. The cursor is a bar in insert mode, a block in command mode,
and an underline in visual mode or while an operator awaits a motion.
.PP
In command mode, arrow keys move between buffer rows without recalling history.
Insert-mode arrow keys recall history. With an operator pending, Up or Down
operates on the current line and the adjacent line. The dk and dj commands
perform the corresponding linewise operations.
.PP
Ctrl-V enters rectangular selection in vi command mode. The d and x keys delete
the rectangle. The I, c, and C keys insert text at the left column of every
selected line.
.PP
Ctrl-V enters multi-cursor mode in emacs mode. Up and Down extend the span. A
typed byte is inserted at every cursor. Backspace erases at every cursor.
Ctrl-G leaves the mode.
.SH JOB CONTROL
An asynchronous pipeline job owns every stage. POSIX stages share one process
group. The final stage supplies $!, the ordinary pipeline status, and the
primary job identity. Pipefail may replace that status with the rightmost
failing stage. Polling, waiting, foregrounding, backgrounding, signaling, and
disowning retain or reap earlier stages.
.PP
A stopped event from any retained stage remains recorded. The
.B wait
builtin returns the first stored stopped status without waiting for that
process again. The
.B fg
and
.B bg
builtins resume every retained stage.
.SH SIGNALS
The main shell ignores SIGPIPE. A forked child restores the default SIGPIPE
action. A producer terminated by SIGPIPE therefore returns status 141.
.PP
An interactive timeout child waits until its process group owns the controlling
terminal. On Windows, a background process receives a new console process group
without a Job Object.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Errors, warnings, and analysis diagnostics include source locations when a
mapping exists. A diagnostic may include a source line, caret, detail location,
and trailing note. The complete source line is highlighted before terminal
clipping.
.PP
Eval, command substitution, function substitution, and process substitution add
their call sites to a source backtrace. A frame is printed once while its source
remains live. A backtrace is omitted when only one synthetic command-line root
exists. A printed frame begins with
.BR trace: .
.PP
Error and warning labels, locations, and messages are bold. Warning labels are
yellow. Note and trace text is cyan. Error carets are bold bright red. Warning,
note, and trace carets are yellow. NO_COLOR disables color.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SHIT_FLAGS
The value supplies default command-line options.
.TP
.B SHIT_HISTORY
The value names the command history file. The default is ~/.shit_history.
.TP
.BR FCEDIT ", " EDITOR
These variables select the editor used by
.BR fc .
FCEDIT takes precedence over EDITOR.
.TP
.B SHIT_DIRECTORY_HISTORY
The value names the z frecency store. The default is
~/.shit_directory_history.
.TP
.B SHIT_CALC_HISTORY
The value names interactive calc history. The default is ~/.shit_calc_history.
.TP
.BR ENV ", " BASH_ENV
These variables select startup files under the conditions in
.BR "STARTUP FILES" .
.TP
.B PATH
The value is a colon-separated command search path. The
.B \-\-clean
option resets it to /usr/bin:/bin.
.TP
.BR HOME ", " PWD ", " OLDPWD ", " CDPATH
HOME supplies home paths. PWD and OLDPWD record the logical current and prior
directories. CDPATH supplies search directories for a relative
.B cd
operand. A relative directory is resolved against logical PWD. The form
.B cd ..
removes the last logical component. A logical PWD is accepted only when it is
absolute and identifies the physical current directory.
.TP
.BR SHELL ", " SHLVL
SHELL names the executable. The invocation path is used when SHELL was unset.
SHLVL is inherited and incremented by a child shell.
.TP
.B IFS
The value controls field splitting and the join performed by "$*". The default
contains space, tab, and newline.
.TP
.BR COLUMNS ", " LINES
An interactive shell stores the initial terminal width and height in these
variables.
.TP
.B PROMPT_COMMAND
The command runs before each interactive prompt. Its status does not replace the
previous command status.
.TP
.BR PS0 ", " PS1 ", " PS2 ", " PS3 ", " PS4
These variables provide the pre-execution, primary, continuation, select, and
xtrace prompts. PS0 defaults to empty. PS2 defaults to "> ". PS3 defaults to
"#? ". PS4 defaults to "+ ". The first byte of PS4 is repeated once per
enclosing subshell. The default PS1 shows the git branch, ahead and behind
counts, user, host, and working directory, ending with a status-colored
bullet that turns bold red on a non-zero exit. The backslash escapes \\u,
\\h, \\H, \\w, \\W, \\P, \\g, \\$, \\?, \\j, \\D, \\., \\t, \\T, \\@, \\A,
\\d, \\s, \\v, \\V, \\!, \\#, \\n, \\r, \\e, \\a, \\[, and \\] are expanded.
The \\. escape emits a bullet, plain when the last exit status is zero and
bold red when it is not.
.TP
.B BASH_XTRACEFD
The value selects the xtrace descriptor. Standard error is used when it is unset
or invalid.
.TP
.BR TERM ", " NO_COLOR
TERM selects terminal capabilities on POSIX systems. A nonempty NO_COLOR
disables prompt, diagnostic, and shitbox cat color.
.TP
.B MANPATH
Completion searches these manual paths for subcommands and options.
.TP
.BR PIPESTATUS ", " REPLY ", " OPTIND ", " OPTARG ", " TIMEFORMAT
These variables hold pipeline statuses, default read and select input, getopts
state, and time formatting.
.TP
.BR SHIT ", " SHIT_VERSION ", " SHIT_COMMIT ", " SHIT_BUILD_MODE ", " SHIT_OS
These values identify the invocation path, version, build commit, build mode,
and build host distribution and kernel.
.TP
.B SHIT_GIT_BRANCH
The repository containing the working directory supplies the branch name. A
detached head supplies a short commit hash. A directory outside a repository
supplies an empty value. An assignment overrides detection.
.TP
.BR SHIT_GIT_AHEAD ", " SHIT_GIT_BEHIND
The repository supplies the unpushed and unpulled commit counts for the current
branch against its upstream. Both are empty outside a repository, with no
upstream configured, or when the count is zero. The common up-to-date case is
resolved from the filesystem without forking. A divergent branch forks
.B git rev-list --count
to count each direction.
.TP
.B SHIT_IDENTITY
The read-only exported value is the lowercase CRC-32 identity of the running
executable. It is computed on first read or before a child starts. An inherited
value is discarded.
.TP
.BR SHIT_WELCOME ", " SHIT_FAREWELL
When set and non-empty, the value is printed once at interactive startup
(SHIT_WELCOME) or exit (SHIT_FAREWELL), prefixed with the shell name. An empty
value prints nothing. When unset, the shell prints the built-in greeting for
the active mood on startup and a fixed farewell on exit. A non-zero exit code
is appended in parentheses to the farewell. A startup file can assign these
to customize the session greeting.
.TP
.B SHIT_ANSI_*
The names BLACK, RED, GREEN, YELLOW, BLUE, MAGENTA, CYAN, WHITE, their BRIGHT_
forms, BOLD, DIM, and RESET supply terminal escapes. A recognized name is empty
when color is disabled. An explicit assignment takes precedence.
.TP
.BR RANDOM ", " SRANDOM ", " SECONDS ", " LINENO ", " EPOCHSECONDS ", " EPOCHREALTIME
These dynamic values supply random numbers, elapsed time, source line, and wall
clock time. They are unavailable in the
.B sh
mood.
.TP
.BR BASHPID ", " PPID ", " UID ", " EUID ", " GROUPS ", " HOSTNAME ", " HOSTTYPE ", " MACHTYPE ", " OSTYPE
These dynamic values identify the current evaluator process, parent, ids,
groups, host, and platform. The value of $$ remains the original shell process
id. These names are unavailable in the
.B sh
mood.
.TP
.BR SHELLOPTS ", " BASH_SUBSHELL ", " BASH_ARGV0 ", " BASH_EXECUTION_STRING ", " BASH_MONOSECONDS
These values expose shell options, subshell depth, argument zero, the current
command string, and a monotonic millisecond count. They are unavailable in the
.B sh
mood.
.TP
.BR FUNCNAME ", " BASH_SOURCE ", " BASH_LINENO ", " BASH_COMMAND
These values describe the active call stack and command. Their array forms
contain the complete stack. They are unavailable in the
.B sh
mood.
.TP
.B BASH_REMATCH
The array contains groups from the last [[ ... =~ ... ]] match.
.TP
.BR BASH ", " BASH_VERSION ", " BASH_VERSINFO
These values advertise a Bash identity. They are seeded when the runtime mood
or an initialized mood is
.B bash
or
.BR bash-posix .
.TP
.BR COMP_WORDS ", " COMP_CWORD ", " COMP_LINE ", " COMP_POINT ", " COMP_WORDBREAKS ", " COMPREPLY
A programmable completion function reads these values and writes COMPREPLY.
COMP_WORDS contains the words on the line. COMP_CWORD is the index of the word
under the cursor. COMP_LINE contains the complete line. COMP_POINT is the
cursor byte offset. COMP_WORDBREAKS contains the bytes that split a word.
.SH EXIT STATUS
The shell exits with the status of the last command it ran. A command path that
names a directory, or has a trailing separator whose target is not a directory,
returns 126. A script file that cannot be opened returns 127. A usage error in a
non-rescue invocation returns 2. A child terminated by SIGPIPE returns 141.
.SH ERRORS
An unavailable command or pathname is reported through its first unavailable
component when that component can be identified. A missing explicit directory
may include one close sibling suggestion. A source caret covers the relevant
component or argument when source mapping is available.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.shit_history
This is the default command history file.
.TP
.I ~/.shit_directory_history
This is the default z frecency store.
.TP
.I ~/.shit_calc_history
This is the default interactive calc history file.
.PP
Startup configuration files are listed in
.BR shit (5).
.SH COMPATIBILITY
The
.B shit
and
.B bash
moods accept Bash syntax in addition to POSIX syntax. The
.B bash-posix
mood selects Bash syntax with POSIX option identity. The
.B sh
mood selects the supported dash-compatible behavior.
.PP
The 0b arithmetic prefix is an implementation extension. Bash writes the same
value with a 2# prefix. The
.BR enable ,
.BR bind ,
and selected
.B help
flags are accepted compatibility forms with the effects described under
.BR BUILTINS .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR shit (5),
.BR bash (1),
.BR dash (1),
.BR sh (1),
.BR fish (1),
.BR busybox (1)
